    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d343-a2">

                <pb facs="0604" n="542" sameAs="frus:frus1878/pg542"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#small-caps">mexican republic, department of foreign
                        affairs.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1877-12-25"><hi rendition="#italic">December</hi> 25,
                        1877</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#italic">New York Herald article of
                        December</hi> 25, 1877.</p>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">pursuit of raiders—troops of the
                        united states and mexicans follow a fresh trail in mexico—good understanding
                        between the soldiers.</hi></p>
                <p>The following has been received in the War Department this afternoon: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p rendition="#right">“<hi rendition="#small-caps">Chicago, Ill</hi>., <hi
                                rendition="#italic">December</hi> 24, 1877. (Received 10.55
                            a.m.)</p>
                        <p rendition="#flush">“General <hi rendition="#small-caps">E. D.
                                Townsend</hi>,<lb/>“<hi rendition="#italic">Washington, D.
                            C.:</hi></p>
                        <p>“The following telegram from General Ord is forwarded for the information
                            of the General of the Army.</p>
                        <p rendition="#right">“P. H. SHERIDAN,<lb/>“<hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Lieutenant-General.</hi></p>
                        <p>“‘Lieutenant Ward was sent to Rio Grande to find trail of horses stolen
                            and driven into Mexico on 15th instant. Has just come in. The Mexican
                            troops got to the river on morning of 20th. Ward showed the Mexican
                            lieutenant the trail, and at his invitation crossed his men and joined
                            the Mexican troops. They followed the trail together all day, going
                            about twenty-five miles. As it had rained heavily, the trail was hard to
                            follow, and another hard rain setting in, they found it impossible to
                            follow it farther. The trail was left about three miles from Newtown,
                            and only six or eight miles below where it crossed the river.</p>
                        <p>“‘Ward says there was perfect good feeling among the Mexican soldiers and
                            our own, and that the lieutenant offered to go with him anywhere he
                            thought the horses could be found.</p>
                        <p rendition="#right">“‘ORD,<lb/>“‘<hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Brigadier-General.</hi>’”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <postscript>
                    <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi
                                    rendition="#small-caps">Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date
                                calendar="gregorian" when="1878-01-12"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                    >January</hi> 12, 1878</date>.</dateline></opener>
                    <p>A copy.</p>
                    <closer><signed>JOSÉ FERNANDEZ,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Chief
                            Clerk.</hi></signed></closer>
                </postscript>
            </div>
